{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# A parent REMOVED in Jira never clears the local parent_id (inbound)\n\nFound while writing the row-13 inbound cell of the J11 mutation table\n(story 5200-e04e-246e-4aae), filed per that story's rule that a cell revealing a\nproduct defect stops and reports. THIS TICKET NOW CARRIES THE FIX (reparented under\nepic e369-a449-4773-48fb by operator ruling).\n\n## Mechanism — TWO independent layers each drop the signal\n\n1. THE SNAPSHOT NEVER CARRIES \"no parent\". The fetcher's parent enrichment merges\n   `client.get_parent_map()` into the snapshot and, when the mapped parent is None,\n   deliberately leaves the field ABSENT: \"When parent_jira_key is None, leave the\n   field absent (top-level issue) - consistent with Jira REST shape\"\n   (src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/fetcher.py:508-511). So an issue whose parent\n   was removed looks identical to an issue that never had one.\n\n2. THE DIFFER REFUSES TO EMIT A CLEAR. `inbound_differ._diff_jira_vs_local` resolves\n   the Jira parent to a local id and emits `changed[\"parent_id\"]` ONLY when that\n   resolution is non-None; the None case is explicitly skipped with the reason\n   recorded inline: \"We do NOT emit parent_id=None to avoid accidentally clearing a\n   locally-set parent when we just can't resolve it yet\"\n   (inbound_differ.py:257-270, helper `_extract_parent_local_id` at :90-110).\n\nThe comment names the real design tension: \"Jira has no parent\" and \"the parent key\nis not bound yet\" are COLLAPSED into the same None, so the differ cannot clear\nwithout risking a spurious clear. The apply layer is already ready for the clear —\n`apply_inbound_records` maps `parent_id` through `lambda v: v or \"\"` and its comment\nsays \"an absent/empty parent maps to '' (clears the parent; ticket 8b25)\"\n(apply_inbound_records.py:361-372). Only the differ never sends it.\n\n## Why this is a gap\n\nStory 5200's row 13 INBOUND oracle is \"`.parent_id` is null\". It is unreachable\ntoday: a de-parenting done in Jira is silently invisible to rebar, so the two\nsystems diverge permanently with no report.\n\nFixing it needs the ambiguity resolved, not just the guard removed: the parent map\nmust distinguish \"queried and absent\" from \"not resolvable\". The information is NOT\nmissing at the source — the map already returns `{key -> parent_key | None}`; it is\ndiscarded at fetcher.py:509-511. **The Approach below resolves exactly this**, and it\nis the reason this is a two-layer change rather than a one-line guard removal.\n\n## Expected failure\n\n`tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py::test_inbound_clear_parent_round_trips`\nasserts `.parent_id` is empty after the DC parent is cleared. It should go RED on\nthe harness until this is addressed. Note that cell asserts the DC-side clear as its\nown SETUP step, so \"Data Center refused to null a sub-task's parent\" is reported\ndistinctly from this gap.\n\n## Approach\n\nTwo layers each drop the signal, so both must change. The design problem is the same one\n`2b16-9be0-a8f5-41d9` faces in the link direction and is solved the same way: an inbound CLEAR is a\nWRITE that destroys local data, so it may only fire on POSITIVE evidence that Jira was asked and\nanswered \"no parent\" — never on the mere absence of data.\n\n**This epic has already settled the predicate shape, in its own story 5200.** That story's first draft\nof the parent-cell wait helper took a predicate over `mapping[key]`, and `get_parent_map` has a\ndegradation contract — any REST failure logs a warning and returns `{}`\n(`adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py:520-527`). So \"no parent for this key\" and \"the parent-map read\nfailed entirely\" were indistinguishable, and the wait would have been satisfied by a broken read. It\nwas fixed before landing to `lambda m: key in m and not m[key]` — **key PRESENT with a falsy value**.\nThat is exactly the distinction both layers below need; the fix is to carry it into production code\nrather than only into the test helper.\n\n### Layer 1 — the snapshot must record \"queried, and there is no parent\"\n\n`fetcher.py:508-511` merges `client.get_parent_map()` and, when the mapped parent is None, deliberately\nleaves the field ABSENT (\"consistent with Jira REST shape\"). Make it explicit instead:\n`snapshot[snap_key][\"parent\"] = None` — key PRESENT, value None.\n\nWhy this is safe for every existing consumer: all of them read the field with `.get(\"parent\")` and\nbranch on falsiness, so an explicit None is indistinguishable from absent to them —\n`inbound_differ.py:104` (`if not parent_raw: return None`), `inbound_fields.py:229`,\n`adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py:519` (`isinstance(parent_raw, dict)`),\n`apply_inbound_records.py:186`, and `adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py:518`. Only the new removal\npath uses `\"parent\" in jira_fields`. **Verify this list against the tree before relying on it** — a\nconsumer that used `\"parent\" in fields` or indexed `fields[\"parent\"]` would change behaviour.\n\nThe loop only visits keys `get_parent_map` returned AND that are in the snapshot, so an issue the map\nnever mentioned still gets NO key — which is the unobserved case, and a total read failure returns `{}`\nand therefore clears nothing. Both degrade safe.\n\n### Layer 2 — the differ must distinguish THREE states, not two\n\n`_diff_jira_vs_local` (inbound_differ.py:257-270) resolves through `_extract_parent_local_id` (:90-110),\nwhich returns None for three different situations collapsed into one, then refuses to emit. Replace the\ntwo-state read with a three-state one:\n\n| snapshot state | meaning | action |\n|---|---|---|\n| `\"parent\"` NOT in `jira_fields` | never queried / read failed / truncated | **skip** — emit nothing |\n| `\"parent\"` present and falsy | queried; Jira has NO parent | **emit the clear** (gated below) |\n| `\"parent\"` present with a key that resolves | Jira parent known | set/compare as today |\n| `\"parent\"` present with a key that does NOT resolve | parent not yet bound | **skip** — retry next pass |\n\nThe existing inline comment (\"We do NOT emit parent_id=None to avoid accidentally clearing a\nlocally-set parent when we just can't resolve it yet\") is correct about the hazard and wrong only in\nthat it treats one unresolvable case as if it covered all of them. Preserve its intent — the\nunresolvable case still skips — while letting the observed-absent case through.\n\n### The gates on the clear\n\n- **MANAGED.** Only clear a parent rebar MANAGED: `should_propagate_removal(\"parent\", local_parent_id,\n  local_ticket)` from `rebar.reducer._managed_refs`. The outbound direction already wraps this exact\n  predicate as `_parent_clear_is_managed` (`adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py:296`), whose docstring\n  states the symmetric intent outright — \"a parent a human set directly in Jira (one local never had)\n  would be clobbered instead of ADOPTED inbound\". Reuse the predicate; do not invent a second rule.\n- **KNOWN BLIND SPOT, recorded not hidden.** `add_managed_ref` is folded by the LINK/parent-set event\n  processors (`reducer/_processors.py:90,411,528`), so a ref is \"managed\" the instant it is set\n  LOCALLY — it does NOT prove we pushed it to Jira. `seed_managed_refs_from_current` compounds this for\n  pre-feature tickets. So MANAGED alone does not establish Jira ever had the parent.\n- **SAME-PASS SUPPRESSION closes that blind spot, and here it already works.** `inbound_differ.py:667`\n  drops any inbound `changed` field the same pass's outbound is writing\n  (`changed = {k: v for k, v in changed.items() if k not in ob_entry[\"fields\"]}`), and the outbound→\n  inbound name translation `_OUTBOUND_TO_INBOUND_FIELD` at :495-498 **does** contain\n  `\"parent\": \"parent_id\"` — verified, so the suppression genuinely fires for parent rather than missing\n  on a name mismatch. A locally-set parent that outbound is pushing this pass is therefore not cleared\n  inbound. **Pin this with a test**, because it is currently load-bearing by inheritance and a future\n  edit to that map would silently remove the protection.\n  Residual window, stated plainly: a degraded pass in which outbound does not write the parent (skipped,\n  disabled, or failed) while inbound proceeds.\n\n### The emit, end to end\n\nEmit `changed[\"parent_id\"] = None`. The apply layer is ALREADY ready: `apply_inbound_records.py:361-372`\nmaps `parent_id` through `lambda v: v or \"\"` under `if _fname in fields`, and its comment says \"an\nabsent/empty parent maps to '' (clears the parent; ticket 8b25)\". So no apply-layer change is expected\n— but **prove it**, do not assume it: the mutation must be followed differ → `InboundMutation.fields`\n→ `run_differs` payload → `apply_inbound` → the reduced ticket's `parent_id` is empty. A `None` value\nis exactly the kind of thing an intermediate `if v` or a falsy-filtering dict comprehension drops\nsilently, which is this epic's signature failure mode (this is how `2b16` turned out to need a second\nfile). If something does strip it, that file joins the scope and the ticket records why.\n\n**Alternative rejected: compare against the previous peer snapshot.** Present-before / absent-now is the\ntextbook signal and would retire the MANAGED blind spot, but `reconcile._write_prev_snapshot_key_set`\npersists `{jira_key: {}}` — key membership only, no fields — as of `6cdd4b00d9`. Using it means adding\na new persisted artifact with its own migration and truncation semantics, larger than this defect\nwarrants.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/fetcher.py` — layer 1 (explicit None for a queried-absent parent).\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_differ.py` — layer 2 + the MANAGED gate. 718/800 LOC;\n  keep the change tight or shed weight along an existing seam, never mechanically.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_parent_clear_88d9.py` (new).\n- **Out of scope:** `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py` (another ticket owns it);\n  `apply_inbound_records.py` unless the end-to-end trace proves the `None` is dropped there.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a parent removed in Jira clears the local `parent_id`: asserted POSITIVELY EMPTY on the reduced\n      ticket, never \"no exception was raised\"\n- [ ] the clear survives END TO END — differ emit → `InboundMutation.fields` → `run_differs` payload →\n      `apply_inbound` → the reduced ticket's `parent_id` is empty. A differ-boundary-only oracle does\n      NOT satisfy this\n- [ ] with the snapshot's `\"parent\"` key ABSENT (unobserved: read failure, `{}` degradation, or a\n      truncated page), NO clear is emitted — simulate the real shape, including\n      `get_parent_map` returning `{}`\n- [ ] with `\"parent\"` present and falsy (queried, genuinely no parent), the clear IS emitted\n- [ ] a Jira parent whose key does NOT resolve to a local id still emits NOTHING (the original guard's\n      intent is preserved and independently tested)\n- [ ] a parent that is not in `managed_refs` is NOT cleared, so a parent a human set directly in Jira is\n      adopted rather than clobbered\n- [ ] the same-pass suppression is pinned by a test: when outbound is writing `parent` in the same pass,\n      the inbound clear is suppressed — asserted through `_OUTBOUND_TO_INBOUND_FIELD` so a future edit\n      to that map fails a test instead of silently removing the protection\n- [ ] steady state does not churn: an issue that never had a parent, and one whose parent is unchanged,\n      emit NO clear on repeated passes\n- [ ] every existing consumer of the snapshot `parent` field is enumerated and shown unaffected by the\n      explicit-None change (a grep-backed list recorded on this ticket, plus a test that a\n      never-parented issue still produces no parent mutation)\n- [ ] every oracle is MUTATION-CHECKED with verdicts recorded on this ticket, and the mutation set MUST\n      include making the clear unconditional — an unguarded clear passes the happy-path oracle while\n      being a data-loss bug, so the guard oracles are the ones that protect the product\n- [ ] `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py::test_inbound_clear_parent_round_trips` is\n      unmodified and turns GREEN on the harness once this lands — UNLESS it reports its explicit\n      `SETUP FAILED` message, which means Data Center refused to null a sub-task's `fields.parent`. That\n      is a PLATFORM finding, not this fix failing; record it and file it separately rather than\n      weakening the cell\n- [ ] `fetcher.py` and `inbound_differ.py` each stay under the 800-LOC cap"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785726718449563001,"uuid":"b91dadeb-7ef8-4d16-b209-b90eaab181bf"}